{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the head outcome recovery design at head 495c02f98dc141b54cd22e68e28eb34989be95e3, tree a12f008e, for request f0bdea6f, ruling f15b9b44 and review request 9a5b5d34. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED, for three bounded changes in documents.\n\nI read the note first, then plan 034, the manifest and the index line. I tested the plan's source claims against this head.\n\nThe design is honest about what the journals can't show, and its choice is sound: a current read can't become an old attempt's answer. The gap is in the question that ruling f15b9b44 added: which further read-only evidence could tell a provider failure from a defect in the host or the observer. The plan answers in general terms and never looks at what the Workers already record.\n\n## Changes requested\n\n1. Name the telemetry that the Workers already write.\n   - The evidence table in \"What further evidence could decide\" opens each row with \"if it already exists\", and the text says that the design \"does not assume those logs exist\". The plan doesn't mention Workers Logs or Analytics Engine.\n   - The checked-in configuration turns both on. `apps/room/wrangler.demo.jsonc:24` enables logs at a sampling rate of 1, with invocation logs off, and line 25 binds `METRICS` to `artroom_metrics_v1`.\n   - `packages/scope/src/metrics.ts:99` writes points indexed by scope ID, with deployment, version, place, end, reason, request, trace and answer. If points exist for the lane scope at the attempt's time, they bear on the table's row on which implementation governed the attempt, and on how a turn ended.\n   - Fix: state which Worker each room ran on. Add one row per sink, saying what it could show for each lane scope and time, what it cannot show, and who may read it. Both are read-only and need no provider probe. I read the writer only. I did not query either sink, so I don't know what they hold.\n   - These sinks keep data for a limited time, so the plan must say so, with the limit for this account. Both attempts ended on or before 30 October.\n\n2. State that a missing diagnosis proves nothing, and name the blind spot.\n   - `packages/scope/src/workspace-host.ts:199` ends `send` with `catch { return null; }`. `Operations.#answer` reports `outside-call-failed` only when the port throws (`packages/scope/src/operations.ts:578`). So an exception inside the head branch reaches no log and no metric.\n   - `ArtifactsProvider` also absorbs failures: `packages/scope/src/artifacts-host.ts:262` returns null when a token can't be read, and line 269 discards a failed Git step's detail.\n   - The plan says that these paths collapse causes. It doesn't draw the two consequences. First, an empty result from item 1's sinks can't clear the provider or the host. Second, a third unknown would be as blind as these two, because the selected reader needs a reply store that doesn't exist and adds no information about a host or provider stage.\n   - Fix: say both. Name a fixed-stage, fixed-name diagnosis at these catch sites as a candidate for the separate implementation request, with the redaction rule of `packages/scope/src/diag.ts`. This head should not design it further. Whether it comes before the reader is the planner's choice.\n\n3. Correct the status lines.\n   - The note says \"Status: draft for formal review\", which becomes false when it lands.\n   - Plan 034's last paragraph says that \"Root can adopt this reviewed note later on an appropriate clean Main\", but this head is that adoption. It also says \"Root\", where the plan means the builder.\n\n## What holds\n\nThese claims match the source at this head:\n\n- `deriveHold` emits `epoch: 1` for open at `packages/derive/src/hold.ts:68`, and `WorkspaceHost` requires epoch 1.\n- `HOLD_ATTEMPTS.head` is 1, and the head rule's `retries` returns true (`packages/derive/src/capability/hold.ts:57` and `:943`).\n- `WorkspaceHost` has no `replies` port. The port itself exists at `packages/scope/src/operations.ts:160`.\n- A timed-out call keeps its promise in memory and offers a valid late answer (`operations.ts:584`).\n- Only an attempt's own answer can follow an unknown (`packages/derive/src/ledger.ts:433-435`).\n- `source-pins.json` holds eight equal files and the three changed files that the plan names.\n\nThe plan keeps the ruling's limits. It releases no room, resend, Check, Continue, provider probe or token operation, and it keeps both rooms stopped. It claims no recovery for either room and infers no cause. Rejecting a raised `HOLD_ATTEMPTS.head` in place is right, because that would change `hold@2` history and replay. The kernel filing section adds nothing to the kernel and leaves later changes to section 7.\n\nI did not recheck the table's request and byte counts for the two rooms against their captures.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The worktree holds this head. `git merge-tree --write-tree` onto main 0f0a949e gives the head tree a12f008e. The head is four documentation paths from main, and the four artifacts match. They read stale because they rest on bases that were retired, which the request records.\n- The evidence verifies. The manifest has SHA-256 d97534f2 and lists all 104 files, which match by SHA-256. The directory holds 2,397,664 bytes, its `SHA256SUMS` has digest de177812 and reruns clean, and no file is writable.\n- check-doc-links on an export of the head: 774 links, 0 broken. `git diff --check`: clean. The three commit subjects have 56, 64 and 61 characters.\n- No gate ran, and the documents-only rule allows that: only documents differ from main.\n\n## Architecture\n\nA host service that supplies provenance to the existing rules is the right place for the reader, and nothing here changes what an outcome means. The contract rests on a store that nobody has designed, and the plan says so. Approved, apart from item 2's note on what a recurrence would show.\n\n## Security\n\nThe design keeps credentials and bodies out of public output and refuses caller-supplied answers. It treats a fresh `ref()` as a provider operation, because it mints and revokes a token. Item 1 should keep that limit: the sinks hold no token by design, and any capture needs the same public-output rule. Approved.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe reader's vocabulary is large for a service that may answer `answer-unavailable` for both rooms. That is acceptable for a design that binds nothing yet, and implementation should revisit it. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved, apart from item 2.\n- Security: approved.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks: the provenance, the one acquisition and one handoff, the preserved reoffers, the stopped rooms, the custody limit and the evidence hold. Ruling f15b9b44's question on further read-only evidence needs items 1 and 2.\n\nA head that makes these changes can come back for a review of its diff from this head. It changes only documents, so the documents-only rule applies.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:96416841dead2e1a55f7797fae0ea7c6e8ea8c8c","binding":"assigned","head":"495c02f98dc141b54cd22e68e28eb34989be95e3","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:04d6fea0a9257be5efa1667282a338b323b0ed22\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:bdf11579925ae3f045796a3bfab3a8b045b25c2e\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1a2f792ab35827655824247219dcf6e56fec9f7f","review_path":"reviewguard@1","stale":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:04d6fea0a9257be5efa1667282a338b323b0ed22, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:364be032963705e2746eb41a8fd37b232d456fb4, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4aa3a00525fb9f500e2c9640ccab369106838cf1, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:96416841dead2e1a55f7797fa